CONTEXT FROM TEXTBOOK:
This figure shows a 3D diagram illustrating the Riemann sum approximation used to define the double integral as a volume. A curved surface z = f(x, y) is shown in xyz-space above a region in the xy-plane; the base is gridded to represent a partition into subrectangles. A single representative rectangular parallelepiped is highlighted in orange: its base is a subrectangle with area Delta_Ak and sampling point (x*_k, y*_k) labeled, and its height is f(x*_k, y*_k), giving volume f(x*_k, y*_k) Delta_Ak. Annotations label Height f(x*_k, y*_k), Volume f(x*_k, y*_k) Delta_Ak, and Area Delta_Ak. The figure introduces the double integral as the limit of a Riemann sum of such parallelepiped volumes over all subrectangles as the partition is refined.

PROJECTION TYPE: perspective
Use the scaffold's true perspective projection after building all geometry. Use this only when the source has real vanishing-point convergence or distance-based size shrinkage; then depth should visibly affect apparent size.

ELEMENTS TO RECREATE IN 3D:
  - Three coordinate axes drawn as black arrows: z pointing up, y to the right, x toward lower-left (viewer), with italic labels 'z', 'y', 'x' at the arrow tips
  - Light-gray translucent horizontal xy-plane quadrilateral (finite square patch) on which the region sits
  - Kidney/bean-shaped closed plane region R in the xy-plane, filled pale teal, outlined with a thick teal-green curve
  - Rectangular grid partition of thin gray lines clipped to the region R (subrectangles of the partition)
  - Translucent teal curved surface z = f(x, y) arching above the region, with a visible darker teal patch marking the part of the surface directly over R
  - Faint light-gray vertical cylinder wall: the boundary curve of R extruded straight up to the surface (the solid whose volume is being approximated)
  - One highlighted orange translucent rectangular parallelepiped (column) rising from a single subrectangle of the base up to the surface, its top face shaded darker orange
  - Orange-shaded base subrectangle of that column lying in the xy-plane (area ΔA_k)
  - Red dot at the sample point (x*_k, y*_k) on the base subrectangle
  - Red dot on the surface directly above, at height f(x*_k, y*_k)
  - Red dashed vertical segment connecting the two red dots through the column
  - Text label 'z = f(x, y)' in the upper right, near the surface
  - Annotation 'Height f(x*_k, y*_k)' at right with a leader arrow pointing to the middle of the dashed vertical segment
  - Annotation 'Volume f(x*_k, y*_k) ΔA_k' at lower right with a leader arrow pointing to the side of the orange column
  - Annotation 'Area ΔA_k' at bottom left with a leader arrow pointing to the base of the column
  - Annotation '(x*_k, y*_k)' at bottom center with a leader arrow pointing to the lower red dot

GEOMETRY NOTES (world-space scale, color palette, and any formula/value needed for correctness — build to these so sizes, zoom, and colors match the source; do not guess dimensions the notes already specify):
World units: xy-plane patch is a square roughly 7 × 7 centered at the origin, drawn in very light gray (#e8e8e8) with a thin gray border. Axes: +Z up to z≈3.5, +Y to the right to y≈3.5, +X toward the viewer's lower-left to x≈3.5, black arrows (#111) with italic labels z, y, x at the tips. The origin sits at the left of the frame; the region R lies in the +x/+y quadrant region of the plane, roughly centered at (1.2, 1.2) with overall span ~3.5 × 3.0 units — it is a kidney/bean shape with a concave indentation on its upper-left (toward the origin), e.g. a closed smooth curve r(θ) = 1.5 + 0.45·cos(2θ) style blob, filled pale teal (#bfe0dc, ~45% alpha) with a 3px teal-green outline (#00a887). Grid lines inside R: thin gray (#9aa5a5), spacing = span/n, axis-aligned, clipped to R. Surface z = f(x,y): sampled over the same xy square, translucent teal (#9ecfcb, ~50% alpha, double-sided), sitting roughly 1.5–3 units above the plane and gently curved (default f = 2 - 0.25x² - 0.25y² gives a shallow dome); scale f so the surface stays between z≈1.2 and z≈3. The darker teal patch is the portion of the surface directly above R (#5fa9a4). The faint vertical cylinder wall is R's boundary extruded from z=0 to the surface, near-white gray (#f0f0f0, ~25% alpha), no outline. The highlighted column: one subrectangle of the partition (default one just right of the region center, about ΔA ≈ 0.55 × 0.55), extruded from z=0 to z=f(x*_k,y*_k); translucent orange (#f5b06a, ~55% alpha) with slightly darker orange top face (#e89a48). Red sample dot radius ~0.08 at (x*_k, y*_k, 0) and a second red dot on the surface at (x*_k, y*_k, f), color #e8372a, joined by a red dashed line (dash ~0.12/0.10). Label placement copies the source: 'z = f(x, y)' upper right above the surface; 'Height f(x*_k,y*_k)' right side with an arrow to the midpoint of the dashed segment; 'Volume f(x*_k,y*_k)ΔA_k' lower right with an arrow to the column's right face; 'Area ΔA_k' bottom-left with an arrow to the column base; '(x*_k,y*_k)' bottom-center with an arrow to the lower red dot. All text black, serif-italic math.

DISCRETE CONTROLS (implement every one of these in #ui, each must work independently):
[
  {
    "id": "surfaceEquation",
    "type": "equation_input",
    "teaches": "The integrand f is exactly what sets the height of every approximating column: change the formula and the surface, the column height f(x*_k,y*_k), and the volume estimate f(x*_k,y*_k)ΔA_k all update together.",
    "label": "Surface z = f(x, y)",
    "fields": [
      {
        "id": "f",
        "label": "f(x, y) =",
        "default": "2 - 0.25*x*x - 0.25*y*y",
        "variables": [
          "x",
          "y"
        ],
        "domain": [
          -3,
          3
        ],
        "role": "integrand"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "samplePoint",
    "type": "drag",
    "teaches": "The sample point (x*_k, y*_k) can be any point of its subrectangle; dragging it moves the column across the region and the readout shows how f(x*_k, y*_k) — and hence the column's volume — varies with position.",
    "teaches_note": "readout shows x*_k, y*_k, f(x*_k,y*_k), ΔA_k and the product"
  },
  {
    "id": "partitionFineness",
    "type": "slider",
    "teaches": "Refining the partition shrinks each ΔA_k, so the columns become thinner and the Riemann sum of their volumes converges to the true volume under the surface — the double integral as a limit.",
    "label": "Partition fineness n (subdivisions per side)",
    "range": [
      3,
      24,
      1
    ],
    "default": 6
  },
  {
    "id": "columnView",
    "type": "toggle",
    "teaches": "Seeing one representative parallelepiped versus the whole family of columns connects the single term f(x*_k,y*_k)ΔA_k to the full Riemann sum over the region.",
    "label": "Show",
    "options": [
      "Single column",
      "All columns (Riemann sum)"
    ],
    "default": "Single column"
  },
  {
    "id": "orbitScene",
    "type": "orbit",
    "teaches": "Rotating confirms the column really stands vertically between the flat base subrectangle and the curved surface, and that the solid is the region swept up to z = f(x, y)."
  }
]

